WEBVTT
NOTE why-deep-sea-creatures-are-creepers - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.923
The blobfish's famous sagging face is
mostly an injury from being hauled up.

00:00:04.923 --> 00:00:10.112
In deep water it is a fairly ordinary,
soft fish. That points to the real answer.

00:00:10.112 --> 00:00:14.051
Deep sea creatures look creepy because
they are built for a place with no

00:00:14.051 --> 00:00:18.151
sunlight, little food and crushing
pressure, and NOAA puts the start of that

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:20.795
sunless midnight zone at about 1,000
metres down.

00:00:20.795 --> 00:00:26.615
The evidence comes from NOAA's depth
zones, years of MBARI vehicle footage and

00:00:26.615 --> 00:00:28.555
Smithsonian specimen work.

00:00:28.555 --> 00:00:34.235
It explains the teeth, lures and red skin.
It cannot settle why they unsettle us.

00:00:34.235 --> 00:00:36.843
Why are deep sea creatures such creepers?

00:00:36.843 --> 00:00:41.494
Start with the light, because almost
everything else follows from it.

00:00:41.494 --> 00:00:44.797
NOAA divides the open ocean into layers by
depth.

00:00:44.797 --> 00:00:49.239
The sunlight zone runs to roughly 200
metres, where there is enough light for

00:00:49.239 --> 00:00:50.104
photosynthesis.

00:00:50.104 --> 00:00:53.574
Below it, the twilight zone fades from dim
blue to nothing.

00:00:53.574 --> 00:00:58.220
By about 1,000 metres you are in the
midnight zone, and from there down through

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:02.337
the abyssal and hadal zones, the only
light is light that animals make

00:01:02.337 --> 00:01:02.984
themselves.

00:01:02.984 --> 00:01:05.475
That is most of the ocean's living space.

00:01:05.475 --> 00:01:10.578
NOAA and the Smithsonian both describe the
deep sea as the largest habitat on Earth,

00:01:10.578 --> 00:01:13.069
and almost all of it is permanently dark.

00:01:13.069 --> 00:01:15.708
Water does not dim all colours equally.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:20.851
Red light is absorbed first, in the upper
layers, and blue travels furthest.

00:01:20.851 --> 00:01:25.862
That single physical fact explains one of
the strangest-looking habits of deep

00:01:25.862 --> 00:01:27.789
animals: many of them are red.

00:01:27.789 --> 00:01:32.783
Shrimp, jellies and some fish carry deep
red pigment, which seems like the opposite

00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:37.596
of camouflage until you remember that,
where they live, there is no red light to

00:01:37.596 --> 00:01:38.077
reflect.

00:01:38.077 --> 00:01:42.339
Red becomes black. Other species skip the
trick and are simply black.

00:01:42.339 --> 00:01:46.729
Smithsonian researchers have described
ultra-black fish whose skin absorbs almost

00:01:46.729 --> 00:01:51.011
all of the light that hits it, so that a
predator's glow or a searchlight finds

00:01:51.011 --> 00:01:52.637
almost nothing to bounce back.

00:01:52.637 --> 00:01:57.507
The colours that look lurid in a
photograph are, at depth, invisibility.

00:01:57.507 --> 00:01:59.895
It feels quiet, but not peaceful quiet.

00:01:59.895 --> 00:02:03.325
More like being somewhere you were never
designed to be.

00:02:03.325 --> 00:02:08.365
If the deep is dark, you might expect
animals to give up on light altogether.

00:02:08.365 --> 00:02:09.786
The opposite happened.

00:02:09.786 --> 00:02:14.953
Researchers at MBARI, the Monterey Bay
Aquarium Research Institute, went through

00:02:14.953 --> 00:02:18.376
years of video from their remotely
operated vehicles.

00:02:18.376 --> 00:02:23.037
They identified each animal on screen and
checked it against what is known about

00:02:23.037 --> 00:02:24.611
which groups produce light.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:29.408
Their result was that about three quarters
of the animals they observed in the water

00:02:29.408 --> 00:02:30.664
column can make light.

00:02:30.664 --> 00:02:36.303
That is not a census of every ocean, and
it relies on classifying whole groups as

00:02:36.303 --> 00:02:37.347
glowing or not.

00:02:37.347 --> 00:02:42.998
But it is direct observation, in place,
over a long record, and it makes the point

00:02:42.998 --> 00:02:43.549
plainly.

00:02:43.549 --> 00:02:48.856
Down here, light is not absent. It is
rare, and it belongs to the animals.

00:02:48.856 --> 00:02:53.512
That is why so many deep sea faces come
with a lamp attached.

00:02:53.512 --> 00:02:58.255
The anglerfish's lure is a modified spine
tipped with an organ full of

00:02:58.255 --> 00:02:59.949
light-producing bacteria.

00:02:59.949 --> 00:03:04.449
In a place where food is scarce and
swimming after it costs energy, it is

00:03:04.449 --> 00:03:06.483
cheaper to make prey come to you.

00:03:06.483 --> 00:03:08.867
Other animals use light the opposite way.

00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:13.636
Many twilight-zone fish have light organs
along their bellies that match the faint

00:03:13.636 --> 00:03:17.475
glow from above, erasing their silhouette
for anything looking up.

00:03:17.475 --> 00:03:21.016
Some shrimp spew glowing clouds to startle
a predator.

00:03:21.016 --> 00:03:25.869
The eerie dots and filaments that make
these animals look alien are each a

00:03:25.869 --> 00:03:30.067
working tool, not decoration: bait,
camouflage, alarm or signal.

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:35.053
The second pressure on deep bodies is
hunger. With no sunlight, there are no

00:03:35.053 --> 00:03:40.599
Most food in the deep arrives from above
as marine snow, a slow drift of dead

00:03:40.599 --> 00:03:45.064
plankton, waste and mucus, or as the
occasional large carcass.

00:03:45.064 --> 00:03:49.507
NOAA describes marine snow as a primary
food source for deep-sea life.

00:03:49.507 --> 00:03:54.661
It is a dependable supply for the
ecosystem as a whole, but for any one

00:03:54.661 --> 00:03:57.565
animal, a real meal can be a rare event.

00:03:57.565 --> 00:04:01.507
Now look at a gulper eel or a viperfish
with that in mind.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:06.784
If meals are rare, you cannot afford to
miss one, and you cannot afford to refuse

00:04:06.784 --> 00:04:08.477
one because it is too big.

00:04:08.477 --> 00:04:11.557
So mouths become enormous relative to the
body.

00:04:11.557 --> 00:04:16.798
Teeth grow long and often curve inward,
working less like knives and more like a

00:04:16.798 --> 00:04:19.877
cage that stops a slippery catch from
escaping.

00:04:19.877 --> 00:04:24.309
Some species have stomachs that stretch to
take prey close to their own size.

00:04:24.309 --> 00:04:26.527
Nothing that arrives can be wasted.

00:04:26.527 --> 00:04:31.469
Many deep fish have weak muscles and
watery flesh, and they spend long periods

00:04:31.469 --> 00:04:33.307
drifting rather than hunting.

00:04:33.307 --> 00:04:38.455
The fangs that make them look ferocious
belong, in many cases, to animals that

00:04:38.455 --> 00:04:40.171
move very little and wait.

00:04:40.171 --> 00:04:44.805
Eyes are where deep animals look most
unfamiliar, and where the evidence is

00:04:44.805 --> 00:04:45.979
clearest about why.

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:50.315
In the twilight zone there is still a
faint glow from above, so many animals

00:04:50.315 --> 00:04:52.427
there have enormous eyes to catch it.

00:04:52.427 --> 00:04:56.955
Deeper, where the only light is
bioluminescence, some species keep large

00:04:56.955 --> 00:05:02.176
sensitive eyes for spotting flashes, while
others have small eyes or have lost them

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:05.509
in favour of touch, smell and the sense of
vibration.

00:05:05.509 --> 00:05:08.542
The barreleye is the case most people
remember.

00:05:08.542 --> 00:05:13.703
For a long time, specimens brought up in
nets suggested an odd fish with tubular

00:05:13.703 --> 00:05:15.445
eyes fixed pointing upward.

00:05:15.445 --> 00:05:20.569
Then MBARI filmed living barreleyes with
remotely operated vehicles and showed that

00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:25.199
the head is covered by a transparent
shield filled with fluid, and that the

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:28.656
tubular eyes can rotate inside it to look
up or forward.

00:05:28.656 --> 00:05:32.395
The two dark spots where you would expect
eyes are its nostrils.

00:05:32.395 --> 00:05:37.019
A creature that looked like a broken
design turned out, alive, to make complete

00:05:37.019 --> 00:05:37.371
sense.

00:05:37.371 --> 00:05:39.818
Some deep animals have gone further still.

00:05:39.818 --> 00:05:44.421
A few dragonfish produce red light from
organs near their eyes, and can see it,

00:05:44.421 --> 00:05:46.635
while most of their neighbours cannot.

00:05:46.635 --> 00:05:50.235
In a world tuned to blue, that gives them
a private searchlight.

00:05:50.235 --> 00:05:55.323
It is a reminder that strange-looking is
often just a sign of solving a problem

00:05:55.323 --> 00:05:57.899
nobody at the surface ever had to solve.

00:05:57.899 --> 00:05:59.705
The third force is pressure.

00:05:59.705 --> 00:06:04.673
NOAA notes that pressure rises by about
one atmosphere for every 10 metres of

00:06:04.673 --> 00:06:09.511
seawater, so near 1,000 metres it is
roughly 100 times what you feel at the

00:06:09.511 --> 00:06:13.317
surface, and it keeps climbing all the way
to the trenches.

00:06:13.317 --> 00:06:18.049
Pressure does not crush a body that is
mostly water, because water barely

00:06:18.049 --> 00:06:21.291
compresses, and there is no air space to
collapse.

00:06:21.291 --> 00:06:26.883
What pressure does is disturb molecules:
it can stop proteins folding properly and

00:06:26.883 --> 00:06:28.656
make cell membranes stiff.

00:06:28.656 --> 00:06:31.870
Deep animals answer with chemistry rather
than armour.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.393
Fish from greater depths tend to carry
more of a small molecule called TMAO,

00:06:36.393 --> 00:06:41.274
which helps proteins hold their shape, and
their bodies often lean towards watery,

00:06:41.274 --> 00:06:43.595
gelatinous tissue with light skeletons.

00:06:43.595 --> 00:06:48.731
That softness is part of why they look
unfinished to us. It is not weakness.

00:06:48.731 --> 00:06:52.139
It is what a body looks like when it is
not fighting the water.

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:55.252
Which brings us to the most famous creeper
of all.

00:06:55.252 --> 00:07:00.419
The blobfish became a symbol of ugliness
from photographs of specimens hauled up in

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:01.104
trawl nets.

00:07:01.104 --> 00:07:05.707
But those images show a fish that has been
dragged out of high pressure into the air.

00:07:05.707 --> 00:07:11.015
Its loose, gelatinous body, built to be
supported by water, slumps and sags once

00:07:11.015 --> 00:07:15.195
that support is gone, and its skin is
often damaged by the net.

00:07:15.195 --> 00:07:20.152
In its own habitat, the blobfish looks
like a fairly ordinary, if soft,

00:07:20.152 --> 00:07:21.269
deep-water fish.

00:07:21.269 --> 00:07:24.613
The face that made it a meme is largely a
decompression injury.

00:07:24.613 --> 00:07:29.888
That is worth holding onto, because it is
not the only case where the creepiness was

00:07:29.888 --> 00:07:31.584
manufactured on the way up.

00:07:31.584 --> 00:07:35.306
Darkness and scarcity also shape how deep
animals meet.

00:07:35.306 --> 00:07:40.517
In a vast dark volume with few
individuals, finding a partner at all is

00:07:40.517 --> 00:07:45.511
Some deep sea anglerfish solved this with
a habit called sexual parasitism, which

00:07:45.511 --> 00:07:48.224
sounds like horror and works like
logistics.

00:07:48.224 --> 00:07:52.383
The males are much smaller than the
females, and in some species a male bites

00:07:52.383 --> 00:07:56.435
onto a female and fuses with her
permanently, their blood supplies joining,

00:07:56.435 --> 00:07:59.568
so that he becomes a source of sperm she
carries with her.

00:07:59.568 --> 00:08:03.515
The Smithsonian has described this among
the anglerfish it studies.

00:08:03.515 --> 00:08:07.520
Not every anglerfish does this, and the
details vary between groups.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:11.272
But it follows the same rule as the teeth
and the lures.

00:08:11.272 --> 00:08:15.493
When encounters are rare, bodies evolve to
make each one count.

00:08:15.493 --> 00:08:18.446
Now the part of the answer that belongs to
us.

00:08:18.446 --> 00:08:23.389
For most of the history of deep sea
science, people met these animals as dead

00:08:23.389 --> 00:08:27.947
specimens, brought up in nets, often
damaged, discoloured or collapsed.

00:08:27.947 --> 00:08:32.639
Photographs were taken under flash, which
lights up red skin that is invisible at

00:08:32.639 --> 00:08:35.131
depth and throws hard shadows across
teeth.

00:08:35.131 --> 00:08:39.644
Close framing hides scale: many of the
fiercest-looking deep fish are small

00:08:39.644 --> 00:08:41.269
enough to sit in your hand.

00:08:41.269 --> 00:08:45.816
And human brains are quick to read faces
into anything with two eyes and a mouth,

00:08:45.816 --> 00:08:50.139
so a gaping jaw reads as aggression even
in an animal that is simply waiting.

00:08:50.139 --> 00:08:53.826
That is changing because of how the deep
is now studied.

00:08:53.826 --> 00:08:59.290
NOAA Ocean Exploration streams live video
from its expeditions, and MBARI has built

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:03.043
a long record of footage from remotely
operated vehicles.

00:09:03.043 --> 00:09:08.084
Seen alive, in their own water, under
controlled light, many of these animals

00:09:08.084 --> 00:09:13.388
look less like monsters and more like what
they are: slow, delicate, finely tuned

00:09:13.388 --> 00:09:14.173
specialists.

00:09:14.173 --> 00:09:18.645
The barreleye and the blobfish are both
cases where seeing the living animal

00:09:18.645 --> 00:09:19.704
changed the story.

00:09:19.704 --> 00:09:23.987
As you watch the footage in this episode,
notice where the light comes from.

00:09:23.987 --> 00:09:28.707
When it comes from the vehicle, you are
seeing the animal as no deep sea neighbour

00:09:28.707 --> 00:09:29.283
ever does.

00:09:29.283 --> 00:09:34.483
When the lights go down and only its own
glow remains, you are closer to its world.

00:09:34.483 --> 00:09:39.309
Notice, too, which shots are of living
animals and which are of specimens.

00:09:39.309 --> 00:09:44.404
A slack body on a deck and the same
species hanging in open water are not the

00:09:44.404 --> 00:09:45.331
same evidence.

00:09:45.331 --> 00:09:49.240
This evidence does not show that every
strange feature is an adaptation.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:53.963
Some traits may be side effects, or
leftovers from ancestors, and for many

00:09:53.963 --> 00:09:58.431
species we have only a handful of
observations, sometimes only damaged

00:09:58.431 --> 00:09:59.069
specimens.

00:09:59.069 --> 00:10:04.212
The MBARI figure on bioluminescence comes
from one region and from classifying

00:10:04.212 --> 00:10:09.091
animals by group, so it is a strong
indication rather than a global count.

00:10:09.091 --> 00:10:12.710
And creepy is not a biological category at
all.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:16.637
Science can explain why a deep fish has
long teeth.

00:10:16.637 --> 00:10:21.138
It cannot settle why a particular viewer
finds those teeth unsettling.

00:10:21.138 --> 00:10:24.739
That part sits in us, and the evidence
there is thinner.

00:10:24.739 --> 00:10:27.216
So the deep sea is not full of monsters.

00:10:27.216 --> 00:10:32.358
It is full of answers to darkness, hunger
and pressure, worked out over a very long

00:10:32.358 --> 00:10:36.136
time by animals that never needed to look
friendly to anyone.

00:10:36.136 --> 00:10:41.091
The creepiness is real as a feeling. It is
just not the animals' purpose.
